# Outbound URLs this site does not check. Read by lychee from the repository root, one
# regular expression per line, matched against the whole URL; anchor each one with ^.
#
# From plabs-house-rules, templates/external-links/. Copy it as .lycheeignore; every line
# below the comments is yours. rules/docs/external-links.md there says when an exclusion is
# right and when it hides a dead link.
#
# Every entry carries a comment saying why and when, because an exclusion nobody can explain
# is one nobody dares remove. A host every site links goes in the template's lychee.toml
# instead, not here.

# This site's own hostname (2026-11-24). Its pages are checked as built, by VitePress itself
# (ignoreDeadLinks: false in docs/.vitepress/config.ts), rather than as deployed: requested on
# GitHub Pages, a link would be checked against the last deploy, not the build in hand, so a
# pull request adding a page would fail on its own new links. VitePress checks relative links
# only, so an absolute link to this host is checked by neither: write internal links relative.
# On 2026-11-24 the absolute ones were the canonical URLs config.ts writes on every page, and
# two examples inside YAML code blocks. The pattern ends with (/|$), so
# a mistyped link such as https://butler.ptarmiganlabs.comdocs/ is still checked and still
# fails. That mistake was on 129 pages until ptarmiganlabs/butler-docs#69.
^https?://butler\.ptarmiganlabs\.com(/|$)

# Slack's Block Kit Builder answers 403 to any checker (2026-11-24). In a browser it redirects
# to Slack's workspace sign-in, so the page is fine for a reader.
^https://app\.slack\.com/block-kit-builder/
